I'll thank everyone at the beginning  then the thanks are done and I would  like to talk to you about money and life  and I think that this follows  beautifully oops  well except that's fine beautifully from  the last presentation by Claire  in thinking about the relationship  between money and life first I'd like to  remind you of something that Claire just  said which is that an economy is social  relations well for me as an  anthropologist money is also social  relations all that money is is a record  of our credit and debts with one another  as anthropologists as diverse as most  and Graber have pointed out so money is  life money is human life many of us in  anthropology of money write about money  as a great record of all of the  transactions that take place among  humans you don't need any material token  to symbolize that all you need is some  system of record-keeping and also at the  origin of the systems of record-keeping  that we see give rise to the thing that  then recognizably becomes money to those  of us in the capitalist West at that  origin point there are living things  like these grains of barley here on the  left  the earliest transactional records on  cuneiform tablets essentially were  marking relations of credit and debt  among people both over barley and  denominated in terms of barley in the  ancient Near East at the mundane level  those sort of early receipts were about  this stuff of subsistence at the  prestige level among those who were  keeping track of those records holding  them in great storehouses and temples  prestige items circulated across the  ancient world that often again index the  materiality of life behind if you will  money so we have objects like this thing  in the middle from Anatolia somewhere  between a thousand and 2,000 BC that's a  oxen cart a prestige item would have  circulated among people of a certain  social status and then on the far right  is a big silver crater a wine vessel it  would have held wine circulating in the  region  that the Phoenicians colonize out to the  west of Europe again the wine a living  thing the kind of coming of the money  that were more familiar with it took  this form comes when the kings and  tyrants as they were called broke up  those ancient hoards of records in  temples and storehouses liberated if you  want to use a contemporary discourse to  think about it liberated silver that had  been accumulating there in the form of  tribute items and started imprinting  with it their own sign stamping with it  the sign of the sovereign you can see  King Darius here on this coin which used  to be in my possession but was stolen  from me in Argentina it's a long story  anyhow but what I find so delightful  about these objects is even though there  was an effort to kind of seal off  clothes off those human social  relationships by cementing it in a coin  and stamping it with the mark of the  sovereign people didn't quite get what  this thing was for and so what did they  do well they adopted the same in in  script oriole culture that they had  before when they were writing out things  on papyrus or clay tablets and marked  these coins with their own mark and you  can see these little punch marks on the  front and on the back but that someone  basically people were endorsing these  things over to one another because they  didn't get that this thing could serve  as a token of value in itself if you're  coming from a world where money is life  this dead object doesn't make any sense  unless you give it new life by endorsing  it over to the next person into whose  possession it it comes now in this  deadening world of this kind of coinage  despite the inscriptions on them hello  there  we have commentators philosophers  Aristotle worrying about this kind of  coming of a new new culture of the coin  and Aristotle seeing this new world many  of you are familiar with the way in  which he decried it and said that this  is in fact  natural thing coin as money seems to  continually reproduce itself in  inanimate nonliving saying making more  of itself and so Aristotle famously  argued against the the making of money  with money as a way to justify the civic  virtues of landed estates and famously  declared money to be unnatural and  against that that order of landed  estates not to say that landed estates  are great by the way right this is  there's no there's no good or bad in the  story it's just what is the Aristotelian  position on money got Christianized and  in the Middle Ages were familiar with  the the Christian discourse that  basically took the Aristotle's language  of money being unnatural and translated  the unnatural into the satanic and so  this is just a close-up of the triptych  you just saw you can see can you see it  you can see the little demons here in  hell and see the but right there there's  a little but pooping pooping money out  and you can see that little coins yeah  so money Satan [ __ ] excretion  money gets deadened in this period and  linked to the earthly but not in the  sense that we were just discussing there  is a counter movement to this of course  and and my tale today is very much of a  dialectic between two different visions  of the life of money to this earthy in  the bad sense money the emerging  mercantilist discourse drew very much on  another metaphor of life the metaphor of  circulation of the blood popularized in  william harvey 1628 book on circulation  which cath Weston has shown feeds right  into political economy of the day  justifying mercantilism and flows of  money as life again not as not as [ __ ]  or as deaf to this circulatory metaphor  though  again in a kind of counter movement the  the physiocrats like Francois kezzeme  here linked money back to the soil back  to the soil against the self-expansion  airy character and qualities of the  emerging capitalism that Harvey  circulatory metaphor animated so I just  want want you to have in your mind these  kind of different notions of the life of  money the life of money as the human  social relationships that then can get  sealed up in a deadened object which  then can get linked to the excretion of  life which can then be revalued again by  the physiocrats as the soil of life the  soil of value with this vision playing  off against that circulatory vision  money as a circulation or money as  finance let's fast forward to Earthrise  and think about the physiocrats  today some of the stuff that Claire was  just talking about in her work finds  expression in some pretty important  foundational essays late in the last  century that were postulating the planet  as the natural limit to growth to  economic growth and you see playing out  now is similar dialectic between finance  and physiocratic motions of money in  ideas of natural capital and of trying  to value so-called ecosystem services  the things that the value that is  generated by the living activity of the  planet so that it can enter into  economic relationships devalued the  accounted for  Robert Costanza in 1997 wrote a germinal  paper on ecosystem services which might  be on the next slide but maybe isn't  well this is from it that postulates  this sort of series of systems and makes  a point that the natural capital of  so-called ecosystem services really  envelops all of the social built in  human capital in in our economy  Costanza and others working in this  trajectory even postulated and this was  for Claire specifically soil capital and  soil money to really think about the  soil functions that then feed into the  generation of added value for human  populations there are a number of ways  in this in which this idea has been  literalized  in proposals for physical currency  objects that derive from the natural  world the Eco was one such proposal by  the next Nature Network and  interestingly then a contemporary  cryptocurrency ties itself to the value  of carbon again think carbon in the  petrochemical petrol industrial complex  world as a kind of death but also  remember that it is the foundation of  life then here in an interesting way  harking back to those ancient coins with  its iconography but taking us into the  carbon cycle as a new means of value now  i it would be wrong to just kind of  leave it at that and not point out that  there are the folks out there who see  any speculation on such alternative  monetary forms as one step closer to  one-world government right so I have a  slide here from technocracy watch which  is what it sounds like they're kind of  worried about you know the the technique  of North America issuing universal  identity cards with chips on them that  are keeping track of your carbon output  and giving you carbon credits and see  carbon currency is the Vanguard for one  world government be that as it may I  actually find some comfort and solace in  our own us banknotes which remained  which are becoming more and more unique  in the world as physical objects because  as they are still made out of the cotton  rag linen fiber blend that we've been  using for around a hundred years they're  interesting reasons why they're unlikely  to be replaced by polymer like you see  in other countries one of the reasons is  that the people who make the money know  that our banknotes American banknotes  serve as a store of value for people all  over the world for a villager in India  who is saving money they're likely to  save us banknotes in a jar in a clay  vessel buried in the backyard  these things need to last linen is  remarkably durable this our our stuff  here has a shelf life undisturbed of  around 30 40 even 50 years because of  its natural composition and there's  another thing I'd just like to point out  before I close that I find delightful  about the new u.s. hundred dollar bill  in designing the security features for  it that that one one of which is that  blue reflective strip the folks at the  Bureau of Engraving and printing  realized that they had at their disposal  a terrific way to make every single one  of these objects unique and the way to  do it was to rely on the qualities of  the natural fibers making up the bill  itself if you if you if you have a  hundred I do not you know that there's  this little strip and there are these  paper bridges that seem to hold the  strip in place the edges of those  bridges are defined by individual flax  fibers in the slurry that makes up the  paper so instead of sort of putting it  through some kind of mechanical process  that then cuts those edges to make them  nice and clean the slurry is allowed to  do however it works someone must be a  paper maker here it does what it does  and route and you get that you get that  that nice wavy thing that nice wavy line  every one of which is different so if  you have a hundred you should look at it  if it's a straight edge it's a fake and  you should call the police okay this is  a way in which  the circulatory notion of money and the  the physiocratic notion of money remains  bonded in the very stuff of American  currency I think there's a lesson in  this banknote for us today and I'll  leave it at that so we have time for  discussion  [Applause]

